// src/v4/extdeps/languages/go.dag
//
// Scope: Go target language spec — primitives, syntax, codegen rules.
// Anchor: https://go.dev/ref/spec — Go Programming Language Specification,
//         **pinned to Go language version go1.26** (the L-2 versioned
//         fidelity authority the carriers below are pinned to, per
//         dsl/extdeps/extdeps.md rule #3 "Declare the version"). The
//         `https://go.dev/ref/spec` URL itself is mutable (it always
//         redirects to the current spec); the pinned VERSION is the
//         authority. When the Specification changes between releases
//         (a new language version adds / renames a primitive, adds a
//         numeric width, adds a visibility scope), the change is a
//         visible C1 edit to BOTH (a) the version pin on this line
//         and (b) the relevant declaration below, never a silent
//         extension. (Pin added 2026-05-16 per cursor/composer-2
//         BLOCKING review on PR #3182 — extdeps fidelity rule #3.)
// Unifies: (per THESIS:188) language spec = transport spec = interpreter
//         runtime. ONE substrate carrier for all three roles. Different
//         lenses read different facts from the same data (emission
//         reads syntax; transport reads serialization; interpreter
//         reads execution semantics). A worker needing transport or
//         runtime extends this file, not a new "transport spec" file.
//
// STRUCTURAL FINDING (this slice's deliverable, manager directive
//   msg_4f7e1280 fan-out — the operator-RATIFIED canonical-rust shape,
//   PR #3174 loyal-koi-702 — applied to Go). Go's TYPE-SYSTEM SURFACE
//   — predeclared scalar vocabulary, channel direction, identifier
//   export-status — fits the substrate's existing classifier shape
//   (closed Disj + Conj records) without modification. No 7th
//   connective, no 6th behavior is surfaced by this slice. The slice
//   declares the Go type VOCABULARY as Node-shaped declaration-class
//   data; it deliberately STOPS at the carrier-shaped seams where D1
//   (parse/emit operations — ratified Outcome<T> = Produced { value:
//   T } | Rejected { diagnostic: Diagnostic }, NOT YET LANDED in
//   std/diagnostic.dag) and D2 (per-language primitive inhabitance
//   instance-values + fn-value-expression form for genuinely-
//   irreducible spec primitives — D2a **resolver** carriers ratified
//   2026-05-16 via the encoded DECISIONS.md D2 row, PR #3195; the D2
//   block below mirrors rust.dag @0328bee951962382aa60143bf563c7b9eb39d215;
//   full numeric alias rows remain substrate-gated per "Owned ELSEWHERE")
//   plug in.
//
//   The seams are NAMED in "Owned ELSEWHERE" below with their owners
//   and dissolution triggers. Authoring those seams here would have
//   improvised past D1/D2 — exactly the rust slice's blessed deferral
//   shape (#3174). Deferral-with-named-trigger, never improvisation.
//
//   Go-vs-Rust per-language fact (the fan-out's deliverable beyond
//   "the shape is the work"): Go's IN-B effect-typing locus is CHANNEL
//   DIRECTION (`chan T` / `<-chan T` / `chan<- T`), not borrow-kind.
//   Go does not have Rust's `&T` / `&mut T` borrow-kind partition;
//   Go pointers (`*T`) carry no aliasing discipline (no shared/
//   exclusive partition — multiple `*T` to the same place is
//   admissible). The structural effect-typing the goroutine/channel
//   concurrency model reads (per the IN-B THESIS:401 framing) is the
//   channel-direction tag on a typed value — Go's analog of Rust's
//   RustReferenceKind, the verilog signal-type, and ptx's StateSpace.
//   The probe's per-language finding: IN-B holds across BOTH borrow-
//   kind (Rust) AND channel-direction (Go) AND signal-type (Verilog)
//   AND state-space (PTX) — the IN-B claim is "effects are intrinsic
//   to the type signature", not "the effect-type carrier is identical
//   across languages". Per-language carriers, one IN-B principle.
//
// Owns (this slice — REVISED from scaffold; scaffold's "Go primitives"
//   + "emission rules" + "cost realization for Go" lines are PRESERVED
//   AS GOALS in the Owns→ELSEWHERE table below; the slice-vs-bundled
//   split is explicit, not silent — same revision discipline as
//   ptx.dag #3170 and rust.dag #3174):
//
//   - Go scalar primitive vocabulary as closed structural carriers:
//     SignedIntWidth (5-way: SignedBits8/16/32/64 + SignedPlatformInt
//     for `int`) and UnsignedIntWidth (6-way: UnsignedBits8/16/32/64
//     + UnsignedPlatformInt for `uint` + UnsignedPointerSized for
//     `uintptr` — Go has no signed-pointer-sized type; the per-
//     signedness width split makes Signed × PointerSized structurally
//     unrepresentable per cursor/composer-2 BLOCKING fix on PR #3182),
//     GoFloatWidth (Bits32/64 — IEEE-754 binary32/binary64 per Go
//     spec §Numeric_types), GoComplexWidth (Bits64/128 — Go's two
//     complex types `complex64` / `complex128`, structurally distinct
//     from the float widths because the complex number is a Conj of
//     two same-width floats), GoScalarKind (the Int/Float/Complex/
//     Bool/String partition per Go spec §Types)
//   - Go scalar typed-shape sum: GoScalar (kind × width as a Disj
//     with named-field variant payloads — illegal kind×width pairs
//     are STRUCTURALLY UNREPRESENTABLE: no "Bool with width=Bits32",
//     because Bool/String variants carry no width payload — the
//     same Disj-with-heterogeneous-payloads design rust #3174 uses,
//     for the same INVARIANTS P2 reason)
//   - Go channel-direction vocabulary: GoChannelDirection (SendRecv
//     / SendOnly / RecvOnly — the Go spec §Channel_types direction
//     partition), and the Conj carrier GoChannel attaching it to an
//     opaque element-type-reference Symbol (K-1)
//   - Go identifier export-status: GoExportStatus (Exported / Unexported,
//     each carrying a `locus: GoExportLocus` payload) PLUS GoExportLocus
//     (PackageBlock / StructField / MethodName — the three
//     export-eligible declaration loci per Go spec §Exported_identifiers).
//     Go's export rule is TWO-CONDITION: an identifier is exported iff
//     BOTH (a) the first character is a Unicode upper-case letter AND
//     (b) the identifier is in an export-eligible locus. The locus
//     payload makes condition (b) structural — function-local
//     declarations carry NO GoExportStatus, so "exported function-
//     local uppercase identifier" is unrepresentable (cursor/composer-2
//     BLOCKING fix on PR #3182). The PARSER reads spelling AND AST
//     locus at the K-1 boundary; kernel/lens code reads the typed tag.
//   - Go per-target cost-realization carrier shape: GoCost (per-
//     instruction + per-allocation components — the per-target
//     projection U1 reads; the carrier SHAPE only, no instance-
//     values pre-T-3 / pre-D2)
//   - Go D2 RESOLVER — **partial fan-out** per T-4 mgr brief (msg_6aaebc89,
//     canonical rust.dag #3174 @0328bee951962382aa60143bf563c7b9eb39d215 +
//     encoded DECISIONS.md D2 row @44a37ad46): mirror **discipline**, not
//     a literal type-copy — Go's spec partition is modeled here (Go ≠ Rust
//     asymmetry). **D2a(1)** alias-identity lands now where kernel-ambient
//     (`Bool` per STRUCTURE.md:147 — std/logic.dag sole authority). **D2a(3)**
//     operation-semantics: Go-only `GoBuiltinIntegerArithmeticOverflow`
//     (silent two's-complement modular truncation on overflow — go.dev/ref/spec
//     §Integer_overflow, pinned go1.26) + `GoIntegerOverflowDisposition<IRCarrier>`
//     — NOT rust's `PanicOnOverflow | TwoComplementWrap` superset (B3 parallel-
//     taxonomy anti-pattern; peer-confirmed (a)). **D2a(2) grounding map** —
//     the ENTIRE facet (shared `GroundingMap<IRCarrier>` type **home** + every
//     `*_grounding` data row) is 🟡 TRACKED-SCAFFOLD **DEFERRED**: operator must
//     pin `GroundingMap`'s single substrate home (P2 — import-never-redeclare-
//     carriers; five identical local declarations across the language fan-out
//     would violate P2). Named trigger = "operator pins GroundingMap's home
//     (under escalation)". Non-kernel-ambient std/ int/float ladder aliases
//     remain 🟡 deferred (explicit `import` when they land; owners std/
//     integer.dag | std/float.dag, T-3 Wave-A3; do NOT mint std/ types here).
//
// Owned ELSEWHERE (deliberately NOT in this slice — named owners +
//   dissolution triggers per the rust #3174 deferral shape):
//
//   - Go scalar inhabitance **instance-values** — D2 RATIFIED 2026-05-16
//     (encoded DECISIONS.md D2 row, commit 44a37ad46 via PR #3195 merge;
//     resolver reshape mirrors rust.dag @0328bee951962382aa60143bf563c7b9eb39d215).
//     The ratified D2a surface is **partial** in this file until D2a(2)
//     pins: alias-identity + operation-semantics carriers in the D2 block
//     below; **grounding-map type + rows are deferred** (operator-pending
//     home — see D2 block). NOT a per-language `data go_<prim>_ring: OrderedRing<
//     GoPrim, …>` inhabitance (INVARIANTS P1:42 `numeric_aliases_align_to_refinements`
//     forbids that parallel-substrate pattern). D2b `Transform →
//     FunctionRef → Arrow` IEEE / complex realization **bodies** ride
//     the bundled T-4 LanguageModel grammar slice per the D2 row's scope
//     guard (same deferral shape as rust.dag). Numeric D2a rows for Go
//     integers, floats, the two complex widths, `string`, and never
//     are 🟡 TRACKED-SCAFFOLD — dissolution triggers in the D2 block's
//     deferred ledger; they amend in when `std/integer.dag`, `std/
//     float.dag`, `std/text.dag`, and related carriers land (T-3 Wave-A3).
//     STOP-TRIGGER (named, binding): authoring even a "placeholder"
//     `OrderedRing<…>` inhabitance row here is the FORBIDDEN pattern
//     (the v3 cross-layer-drift failure mode this file's restraint exists
//     to prevent). The PR #3171 improvisation-held draft is the precedent
//     for what NOT to do. Stub-to-keep-moving is forbidden; defer-with-
//     named-trigger is the legitimate form.
//     SEAM (where it plugs in): the closed GoScalar Disj declared below
//     is the SURFACE through which each Go scalar's inhabitance
//     parameterizes — when upstream std/ carriers land, the per-scalar
//     D2a rows amend in with the typed scalar variants below as their
//     carriers. Special inhabitance notes:
//       * Go's `complex64` / `complex128` inhabit a field-like
//         structure (`ApproximateField` for the rounding-aware
//         analog — IEEE-754 binary32/binary64 component pairs
//         compose into a complex number whose multiplication is
//         rounding-approximate), specifically `ApproximateField<
//         Conj<Float, Float>>` or a richer complex carrier in
//         std/float.dag's eventual extension. Not authored here.
//       * Go's `string` inhabits FreeMonoid<Byte> (UTF-8 bytes —
//         Go strings are immutable byte sequences, NOT necessarily
//         valid UTF-8 per the spec, though idiomatic Go strings
//         are UTF-8; FreeMonoid<Byte> is the faithful inhabitance,
//         with the UTF-8 interpretation as a SEPARATE lens that
//         reads byte sequences). Owned by std/text.dag's eventual
//         inhabitance plus std/machine.dag's Byte carrier.
//       * `byte` (alias for `uint8`) and `rune` (alias for `int32`)
//         are NOT separate scalar kinds — the Go spec defines them
//         as aliases (§Numeric_types), so per P2 they ground in
//         the existing `UnsignedIntScalar { width: UnsignedBits8 }`
//         (for `byte` ≡ `uint8`) and `SignedIntScalar { width:
//         SignedBits32 }` (for `rune` ≡ `int32`) carriers
//         respectively. The alias names live in the boundary
//         intern table; kernel/lens code reads the underlying
//         GoScalar.
//
//   - Bidirectional Go LanguageModel substrate (grammar productions
//     as declarative B2-OMNI data, C5-fidelity disposition tags per
//     surface feature, ingest∘emit roundtrip carrier): the BUNDLED
//     work of T-4 across all 5 Shape A source languages, where "the
//     SHAPE is the work" (TASKS.md T-4 authoring contract). RIDES
//     the operator D1 value-or-Diagnostic carrier — `Outcome<T> =
//     Produced { value: T } | Rejected { diagnostic: Diagnostic }`,
//     RATIFIED 2026-05-16 (manager reinforcement msg_8b477982,
//     ratified shape), NOT YET LANDED in std/diagnostic.dag. Parse /
//     emit operations cannot type their failure mode without it.
//     When the carrier lands AND the bundled LanguageModel shape
//     lands, this file's classifiers slot into the Go `LanguageModel`
//     instance without revision.
//     STOP-TRIGGER (named, binding): the temptation to STUB an
//     `emit_go : Node -> Outcome<GoSource>` op here while D1 is
//     unlanded is the FORBIDDEN deferral — STOP and surface, never
//     stub. Stub-to-keep-moving is the exact shape that broke v3.
//
//   - Pipeline emission/ingest operations (`.dag` Node → Go source;
//     Go source → Node ingest with C5-fidelity classification per
//     surface feature). RIDES the ratified `Outcome<T>` D1 carrier
//     directly. Owners: compiler/05_emit.dag (T-10), compiler/
//     02_parse.dag (T-7). Defer-with-named-trigger, never stub.
//
//   - Goroutine + channel-coordination semantic enforcement (the
//     send/receive direction-vs-operation legality rules — a
//     SendOnly channel rejects `<-ch` receives, a RecvOnly channel
//     rejects `ch <- v` sends, the close-vs-send race avoidance):
//     the COORDINATION-EFFECT-TYPING surface (lens/effect.dag T-13
//     and/or extdeps/coordination.dag T-4.8) CONSUMES the
//     `GoChannel` carrier declared here as the structural fact it
//     reads. Per THESIS:401 IN-B "effects are intrinsic to the type
//     signature, not an annotation layer" — channel direction is a
//     STRUCTURAL Go fact, not an annotation; declared here as the
//     type-system surface and READ by the effect/coordination lens
//     from the signature.
//
//   - Goroutine intra-program concurrency (`go f(args)` spawning
//     parallel sibling computations): the same Conj-of-parallel-
//     siblings + per-goroutine Bind chain shape as verilog #3176's
//     always-blocks finding. A goroutine's body is a Transform
//     invoked as a structural sibling in the enclosing scope's
//     statement list; the M:N runtime scheduler is the mechanical
//     realization of the dependency graph (THESIS:18 "parallelism
//     is the default — independence is visible in the structure").
//     The `go` keyword is a structural decoration on a call site,
//     not a 6th `Goroutine` behavior. The structural carrier for
//     "this call site is a goroutine" lives at the LanguageModel
//     grammar layer (T-4 bundled work) — not the scalar/channel
//     vocabulary layer this slice owns.
//
//   - Interface declarations (`type X interface { Method() }`),
//     struct types, slice / map / array types, function types —
//     the COMPOSITE TYPES partition of the Go spec. Modeled at the
//     bundled LanguageModel grammar layer; the scalar vocabulary
//     here is the irreducible carrier the composite types compose
//     over. Same scoping as ptx.dag's "scope negative warp
//     intrinsics + tensor cores" and rust.dag's "scope negative
//     tuple types + generics".
//
//   - Go per-instruction cost INSTANCE-VALUES (concrete `GoCost {
//     instruction_cost: 1, allocation_cost: 0 }` rows per operation):
//     the carrier SHAPE is owned here (GoCost below); concrete cost
//     rows ride T-12 lens/cost.dag + U2.
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/node.dag, std/algebra.dag (same as rust.dag)
//
// KERNEL-AMBIENT traceability (STRUCTURE.md:147 — usable without import;
// std/ file remains sole modeling authority; listed here for header
// traceability only): `Bool` (D2a(1) `GoBool = Bool` — std/logic.dag),
// `Int` (GoCost instruction/allocation axes — std/integer.dag).
//
// Status: T-4 go-slice modeled 2026-05-16 (bright-crane-527);
//   structural finding: NO STOP from the type-system surface. The
//   I/O contract in this header is immutable for downstream workers.
//   The scaffold's `Consumes` line — `std/node.dag, std/algebra.dag
//   (same as rust.dag)` — is preserved verbatim. The effective
//   imports this slice declares are narrower: only `Symbol` from
//   std/node.dag (the K-1 opaque kernel identity used as the
//   channel-element-type-reference on `GoChannel`). std/algebra.dag
//   is NOT imported because the inhabitance instance-values this
//   slice would otherwise declare ride full numeric D2a alias rows
//   (substrate-gated) — the import returns when std/integer.dag /
//   std/float.dag land and the per-scalar inhabitance instance-values
//   here, exactly the rust #3174 pattern. This per-file Consumes
//   correction rides the systemic operator scaffold-Consumes
//   reconciliation (T-4 mgr synthesis msg_1fddb75c) — uniform with
//   ptx #3170, spice #3168, llvm_ir #3171, rust #3174, and verilog
//   #3176; the operator ratifies all per-file Consumes strikes
//   uniformly in one pass; this is not worker-settled. Splitting
//   this file requires explicit operator ratification (substrate
//   extension = stop signal).
// Brief: see BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md; this file's I/O contract is the immutable
// contract for the worker. Splitting this file requires explicit operator
// ratification (substrate extension = stop signal).


module v4.extdeps.languages.go


import v4.std.node { Symbol }
// Bool from v4.std.logic is KERNEL-AMBIENT (STRUCTURE.md "Kernel-
// ambient types") — usable without an import statement in any v4
// .dag file. The D2a alias-identity row `type GoBool = Bool` below
// references the type-parameter `Bool` directly; v2-compiler resolves
// it ambiently (same pattern as rust.dag @0328bee951962382aa60143bf563c7b9eb39d215).


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes (read before validating against the anchor)
//
// THE SLICE-VS-BUNDLED FRAMING (T-4, manager directive fan-out from
// the operator-RATIFIED canonical-rust shape #3174). T-4 is the bundled
// work across the 5 Shape A source languages: "the SHAPE is the work"
// — the canonical declarative LanguageModel shape was established on
// rust.dag; THIS slice is the go HALF of fan-out: the type-system
// VOCABULARY and Go-side carrier shapes, with the D1/D2-dependent
// pieces (parse/emit operations + scalar algebra inhabitance instance-
// values) explicitly DEFERRED to their named owners. Deferral ≠
// improvisation: every deferred item is named in "Owned ELSEWHERE"
// with its owner and dissolution trigger.
//
// THE GO → SUBSTRATE STRUCTURAL FIT. Each Go surface concept maps to a
// member of the closed substrate axes (node.dag's 6 connectives + 5 L1
// behaviors). The mapping, member-by-member — recording it here
// because the fit IS the deliverable (per the slice's structural
// finding above):
//
//   - FUNCTION (`func name(args) (returns) { body }`) ↦ Transform.
//     Spec: https://go.dev/ref/spec#Function_declarations.
//     A Go `func` is a typed function — `(args) -> (returns)` — whose
//     body is a sub-DAG of behaviors. That is exactly Transform (the
//     typed-total-λ fragment INVARIANTS:72; node.dag:209 "Transform =
//     application"). Multiple return values are modeled as a Conj-
//     record return type (the bundled LanguageModel grammar layer
//     authors the multi-return Conj when it lands).
//
//   - VAR / SHORT-VAR DECLARATION (`var x T = expr;`, `x := expr;`)
//     ↦ Bind. Spec: https://go.dev/ref/spec#Variable_declarations and
//     https://go.dev/ref/spec#Short_variable_declarations. A Go `var`
//     or `:=` introduces a name into a scope with a value; that is
//     exactly Bind (node.dag:209 "Bind = let"). Go variables are
//     mutable by default (unlike Rust's `let` requiring `mut` for
//     rebinding); the mutability is a BINDING-LEVEL fact distinct
//     from any TYPE-LEVEL discipline (Go has no `&mut` analog — see
//     POINTER below).
//
//   - SWITCH / TYPE-SWITCH (`switch x { case … }`, `switch x.(type)
//     { case T1: … }`) ↦ Branch. Spec: https://go.dev/ref/spec#Switch_statements.
//     A Go switch is a guarded sum over a discriminator — exactly
//     Branch (node.dag:209 "Branch = sums"). Go's case-fallthrough
//     semantics (fall-through is opt-in via the `fallthrough`
//     keyword, NOT the default) is a SCHEDULING property of the
//     Branch's arms, modeled at the LanguageModel grammar layer.
//     Type-switch (`switch v := x.(type) { case T1: …; case T2: …;
//     default: … }`) is a Branch over the EXPLICIT case-type list
//     authored at the switch site, matching the dynamic type of `x`
//     against each case-type per Spec §Switch_statements (NOT a
//     Branch over the closed set of concrete types implementing the
//     interface — corrected 2026-05-16 per cursor non-blocking
//     finding on PR #3182; Go type-switches match against the
//     site-authored case list, with `default` covering the residual.
//     The closed-implementing-type framing is a SEPARATE, optional
//     exhaustiveness analysis the LanguageModel grammar lane may
//     perform on closed interfaces; it is NOT how the type-switch
//     itself dispatches per spec).
//
//   - FOR LOOP (`for init; cond; post { … }`, `for cond { … }`,
//     `for x := range y { … }`) ↦ Loop with BOUNDED descent
//     (node.dag:42-68, the A2 discipline). Spec: https://go.dev/ref/spec#For_statements.
//     `for x := range y` is bounded by `y`'s iterator cardinality (a
//     finite measure when `y` is bounded — slice / array / map /
//     channel-with-close / string); `for cond` is bounded by a
//     ranking dimension that decreases on each iteration; `for { }`
//     with no `break` is structurally unbounded — STOP under A2 (the
//     substrate refuses to claim totality it cannot prove). The v4
//     Go model reads bounded loops, fail-closes on unbounded ones.
//
//   - LITERAL / VALUE (`42`, `"hello"`, `true`, `3.14`, `2 + 1i`) ↦
//     Value (node.dag:209). Spec: https://go.dev/ref/spec#Constants.
//     A literal IS a Value. The literal's typed interpretation (its
//     scalar kind + width for numerics; its UTF-8 / byte
//     interpretation for strings; its component widths for
//     complexes) is the inhabitance walk that RIDES D2 — pre-D2 the
//     Value's TYPED interpretation is unspecified; the Value carrier
//     itself is just a Node. Untyped-constant promotion (Go's
//     idiomatic numeric-literal handling per spec §Constant_expressions)
//     is a LanguageModel-layer concern.
//
//   - POINTER (`*T`, `&x` addressing) — NO REFERENCE-KIND PARTITION
//     (the explicit Go-vs-Rust difference). Spec: https://go.dev/ref/spec#Pointer_types.
//     Go pointers are uniform: a `*T` is just a memory address. There
//     is no shared/exclusive partition, no borrow-checker, no lifetime
//     parameter — multiple `*T` to the same place is admissible at all
//     times. The substrate carrier for "this typed value is reached
//     through a pointer" is a Conj record at the LanguageModel grammar
//     layer (a PointerType with a pointee element-type-ref), NOT a
//     reference-kind classifier at this slice's vocabulary layer.
//     This is the structural Go-vs-Rust per-language fact: same
//     IN-B principle (effects intrinsic to type signature), different
//     effect-typing locus — Go's coordination-discipline locus is
//     CHANNEL DIRECTION (below), not pointer-kind.
//
//   - CHANNELS (`chan T`, `<-chan T`, `chan<- T`) ↦ effect-typed
//     parameter (structural Conj field), per THESIS:401 IN-B applied
//     to Go. Spec: https://go.dev/ref/spec#Channel_types. A channel
//     is NOT a separate behavior; it is a TYPE whose
//     `direction: GoChannelDirection` and `element_type_ref: Symbol`
//     fields are structural Conj coordinates. Channel I/O (`ch <- v`
//     send, `<-ch` receive) is a Transform whose operand type
//     CARRIES the channel direction in its signature; the effect /
//     coordination lens (T-13 / T-4.8) READS that signature and
//     validates well-typedness (a SendOnly channel rejects receives,
//     a RecvOnly channel rejects sends) as a structural property.
//     This is EXACTLY the rust #3174 RustReference precedent and
//     the ptx #3170 RegisterScalar.state_space precedent, applied
//     to Go's channel-direction locus. Tempting alternative: a
//     `ChannelSend` / `ChannelRecv` behavior wrapping every channel
//     op. REJECTED — that would parallel-represent what the
//     Transform's signature already says; the effect-typed parameter
//     is the unannotated primitive (feedback_no_annotations).
//
//   - GOROUTINES (`go f(args)`) ↦ Conj-of-parallel-siblings (per the
//     verilog #3176 IN-B finding). Spec: https://go.dev/ref/spec#Go_statements.
//     A `go` statement spawns a parallel sibling computation: the
//     called function is a Transform invoked as a structural sibling
//     in the enclosing scope's statement list (same shape as
//     verilog's parallel `always`-blocks in a module body). The M:N
//     goroutine runtime is the mechanical realization of the
//     dependency graph (THESIS:18); the `go` keyword is a STRUCTURAL
//     decoration on the call site indicating "execute concurrently
//     with the next statement", not a new behavior. NO 6th `Goroutine`
//     behavior; intra-program Go concurrency = Conj-of-parallel-
//     siblings + per-goroutine Bind chain + channel-effect-typed
//     coordination. Same per-language IN-B win as verilog: structural
//     parallelism + per-language effect-typing carriers.
//
//   - DEFER (`defer f()`) ↦ Bind with deferred-scheduling discriminator.
//     Spec: https://go.dev/ref/spec#Defer_statements. A `defer`
//     statement schedules a function call for execution at the
//     enclosing function's return — exactly Bind with a scheduling
//     discriminator on the sequencing-point (analogous to verilog's
//     ProceduralAssignKind = NonBlocking, which schedules a write
//     for the end of the simulation time-step). The scheduling
//     discriminator is a LanguageModel-grammar-layer fact; not
//     authored at this vocabulary slice.
//
//   - METHOD (`func (r Recv) Method() …`) ↦ Transform with a named
//     receiver argument. Spec: https://go.dev/ref/spec#Method_declarations.
//     A Go method is a function with an extra implicit "receiver"
//     parameter; the receiver's type is the named type the method is
//     defined on. Structurally a Transform. Interface method dispatch
//     (`iface.Method()`) is ALSO a Transform — function application
//     whose CALLEE is dynamically selected by the receiver's dynamic-
//     type method-set lookup per Spec §Method_sets +
//     §Interface_types. This is NOT a Branch (corrected 2026-05-16
//     per cursor BLOCKING finding on PR #3182): Go interfaces are
//     duck-typed (structurally implemented), so the implementing-
//     type set is OPEN — ANY non-interface type whose method-set is
//     a superset of the interface's method-set qualifies, and the
//     set ranges over the universe of declarable types. A closed-
//     Branch model violates INVARIANTS P1 (the spec partition is
//     OPEN, not closed) and P4 (the implementing-type set is not
//     decidable at compile time without whole-program analysis).
//     The structurally honest model: a virtual-dispatch Transform
//     whose function value is the method-set table entry resolved
//     at the call site from the receiver's dynamic type — same
//     shape as a function-pointer call. The dynamic method-set
//     lookup itself is owned by the bundled T-4 LanguageModel
//     grammar + runtime productions; this vocabulary slice declares
//     only the Transform structure.
//
// CONCLUSION. The type-system half of Go's spec fits the existing
// substrate with no new connectives, no new behaviors, no parallel
// taxonomies. The classifiers declared below are the closed Go-side
// surface; their composition with grammar productions (Owned
// ELSEWHERE) and inhabitance instance-values (Owned ELSEWHERE — rides
// D2) lands when those dependencies dissolve, with the I/O contract
// of THIS file immutable.
//
// SCOPE NEGATIVE (operator-tier, do not work around).
//   - Composite types (slice `[]T`, array `[N]T`, map `map[K]V`,
//     struct, function-type, interface) — Go spec §Types partitions
//     them as composite. Modeling each requires generic carriers
//     (Conj over component types) at the LanguageModel grammar layer
//     (T-4 bundled work); not authored here. Same scoping as rust.dag
//     #3174's "tuple types out-of-scope" and ptx.dag #3170's "warp
//     intrinsics out-of-scope".
//   - The full Go GRAMMAR production set (declarations, expressions,
//     statements, type-switch patterns, package-import syntax) is
//     owned by the bundled T-4 LanguageModel shape. Not authored
//     here.
//   - Generics (type parameters introduced in Go 1.18 per the
//     spec's §Type_parameters section). Modeled at the LanguageModel
//     grammar layer; structural inhabitance against std/algebra.dag
//     algebras (Go's `comparable` constraint, type-set constraints)
//     rides D2.
//   - The Go runtime + garbage-collector — the cost-realization
//     carrier here (GoCost.allocation_cost) names the seam through
//     which GC pressure enters the cost lens, but the GC's internal
//     model (mark-sweep generations, write barriers, stop-the-world
//     pauses) is a per-runtime-implementation detail, not a
//     language-spec fact; the lens reads only the structural
//     "this operation allocates" fact, not the GC's internal state.
//   - `unsafe.Pointer` and the `unsafe` package — Go's escape-hatch
//     surface (Spec §Package_unsafe). A STRUCTURAL boundary fact (a
//     declaration / call-site carries an unsafe-package discriminator),
//     modeled at the LanguageModel grammar layer when the bundled
//     work lands; not the vocabulary slice.
//   - Reflection (`reflect` package) — a library, per L-2 (a library
//     is just a program in the modeled language = Node); not a
//     language-spec feature requiring substrate.
//
// PRACTICE-4 LEDGER DISCIPLINE. Every closed-Disj carrier below
// carries the full 🟢/🟡/🔴 + all five patterns (Fact-placement /
// Variant-is-data / Algebraic-form / Dimensional / Parameterized-
// family-Practice-7) per modeling-discipline.md §4 (operator-ratified
// binding 2026-05-15, gate (b) — #3163 made the 4-pattern miss the
// proof case; do not omit Practice-7). Conj records carry no ledger
// (only coproducts dissolve); scaffold-residual Conj fields (GoCost
// raw Int components) carry a 🟡 bridge note mirroring std/diagnostic
// .dag's `Extent.ByteRange`, ptx.dag's `Dim3` / `PtxCost`, and
// rust.dag's `RustCost` precedent.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// SignedIntWidth — the closed signed-integer width classifier. The Go
// Specification §Numeric_types admits exactly five signed widths:
// `int8`, `int16`, `int32`, `int64`, and the platform-dependent `int`.
// There is NO signed-pointer-sized type in Go (`uintptr` is unsigned-
// only) — that asymmetry is the per-language fact this slice's
// dissolution carries (cursor/composer-2 BLOCKING review on PR #3182
// 2026-05-16: the prior `IntScalar { kind: GoIntKind, width: GoIntWidth }`
// Cartesian-product carrier admitted `Signed × PointerSized` which has
// no Go analogue — INVARIANTS P2 illegal-states-unrepresentable
// anti-pattern. Splitting widths per signedness makes the missing
// Signed × PointerSized cell structurally unrepresentable, not
// "rejected by a check").
// Spec: https://go.dev/ref/spec#Numeric_types.
//
//   - SignedBits8         — `int8`   (1-byte signed)
//   - SignedBits16        — `int16`  (2-byte signed)
//   - SignedBits32        — `int32`  (4-byte signed; `rune` is an
//                                     alias for `int32` and grounds
//                                     on this variant per Owned
//                                     ELSEWHERE)
//   - SignedBits64        — `int64`  (8-byte signed)
//   - SignedPlatformInt   — `int`    (platform-dependent default
//                                     signed integer width; per spec
//                                     ≥ 32 bits, typically 64 on
//                                     64-bit platforms)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A 128-bit signed width (Go has no stable
// int128 in the pinned Specification — distinct from Rust's i128) is
// an edit to THIS declaration under the L-2 pin discipline.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every signed-integer-scalar consumer
//      (emission of `int32` vs `int64`; per-target cost-realization
//      sizes; the eventual OrderedRing inhabitance walk) reads the
//      width tag uniformly; shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ bits: Int }` proxy (8/16/32/
//      64/<platform>) replaces a closed 5-valued exhaustive sum
//      with an unbounded Int (bits=42 representable), AND fails to
//      express SignedPlatformInt as a STRUCTURALLY platform-dependent
//      kind. The widths are NOT free Int values; they are the
//      Go-Specification-admitted signed subset, so a free-Int
//      carrier would ratify illegal widths (P2 illegal-states-
//      unrepresentable shape, same as ptx.dag RegisterWidth).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the five widths
//      structurally inhabit the SAME algebra (OrderedRing — signed
//      two's-complement); they do not partition into distinct
//      algebraic structures. The inhabitance ride D2 per "Owned
//      ELSEWHERE".
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single typed signed integer has
//      EXACTLY ONE width; mutually-exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the five widths are the Go-
//      stable-signed-integer set; they are NOT one generic `F<X>` /
//      projection over a separately-declared source set (Practice 7).
//      The fixed widths' bit-count is the discriminant for the
//      fixed variants; SignedPlatformInt is a different-shaped
//      variant (platform-dependent, not a fixed count) — they are
//      not all `F<bit-count: Int>`.
// Terminal: the closed five-way Go signed-integer-width classifier
// for the pinned Go Specification.
type SignedIntWidth
  = SignedBits8
  | SignedBits16
  | SignedBits32
  | SignedBits64
  | SignedPlatformInt


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// UnsignedIntWidth — the closed unsigned-integer width classifier.
// The Go Specification §Numeric_types admits exactly six unsigned
// widths: `uint8`, `uint16`, `uint32`, `uint64`, the platform-
// dependent `uint`, and the pointer-sized `uintptr`. The asymmetry
// vs SignedIntWidth (six unsigned widths vs five signed) is the
// per-language fact this slice surfaces: `uintptr` exists but
// `intptr` does not — there is no signed-pointer-sized type in Go.
// This structural fact is faithfully reflected by giving UnsignedInt-
// Width one more variant than SignedIntWidth (rather than collapsing
// the Cartesian product across a shared GoIntKind × GoIntWidth pair
// the way Rust's RustIntKind × RustIntWidth would suggest — Rust has
// symmetric isize/usize, Go does not).
// Spec: https://go.dev/ref/spec#Numeric_types.
//
//   - UnsignedBits8         — `uint8`  (1-byte; `byte` is an alias
//                                       for `uint8` and grounds on
//                                       this variant per Owned
//                                       ELSEWHERE)
//   - UnsignedBits16        — `uint16` (2-byte unsigned)
//   - UnsignedBits32        — `uint32` (4-byte unsigned)
//   - UnsignedBits64        — `uint64` (8-byte unsigned)
//   - UnsignedPlatformInt   — `uint`   (platform-dependent default
//                                       unsigned integer width; per
//                                       spec ≥ 32 bits, paired with
//                                       SignedPlatformInt)
//   - UnsignedPointerSized  — `uintptr` (unsigned integer large
//                                        enough to hold the bit
//                                        pattern of any pointer;
//                                        structurally DISTINCT from
//                                        UnsignedPlatformInt per spec
//                                        — the spec does not equate
//                                        them even on platforms where
//                                        they happen to be the same
//                                        width)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A 128-bit unsigned width is an edit to
// THIS declaration under the L-2 pin discipline (Go has no stable
// uint128 in the pinned Specification).
//
// SPEC FAITHFULNESS NOTE (INVARIANTS P1). The Go Specification
// §Numeric_types preserves `uint` and `uintptr` as STRUCTURALLY
// DISTINCT types (the spec does not equate them). UnsignedPlatformInt
// and UnsignedPointerSized are separate variants reflecting this.
// Grounding the partition in the spec partition is what INVARIANTS
// P1 modeling-faithfulness requires.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every unsigned-integer-scalar consumer
//      reads the width to size representations (emission of `uint32`
//      vs `uint64` vs `uintptr`; per-target cost-realization sizes);
//      shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ bits: Int }` proxy replaces a
//      closed 6-valued exhaustive sum with an unbounded Int, AND
//      fails to express UnsignedPlatformInt vs UnsignedPointerSized
//      as STRUCTURALLY DISTINCT platform-dependent kinds (P2
//      illegal-states-unrepresentable shape).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the six widths
//      structurally inhabit the SAME algebra (Semiring — unsigned
//      modular); they do not partition into distinct algebraic
//      structures. The inhabitance ride D2 per "Owned ELSEWHERE".
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single typed unsigned integer has
//      EXACTLY ONE width; mutually-exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the six widths are the Go-
//      stable-unsigned-integer set; they are NOT one generic
//      `F<X>` / projection over a separately-declared source set
//      (Practice 7). The fixed widths' bit-count is the discriminant
//      for the fixed variants; UnsignedPlatformInt and Unsigned-
//      PointerSized are DIFFERENT-SHAPED variants (platform-
//      dependent, not a fixed count, AND structurally distinct from
//      each other per spec) — they are not all `F<bit-count: Int>`.
// Terminal: the closed six-way Go unsigned-integer-width classifier
// for the pinned Go Specification.
type UnsignedIntWidth
  = UnsignedBits8
  | UnsignedBits16
  | UnsignedBits32
  | UnsignedBits64
  | UnsignedPlatformInt
  | UnsignedPointerSized


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// GoFloatWidth — the closed Go floating-point bit-width classifier.
// The Go Specification §Numeric_types admits exactly two widths (both
// IEEE-754 binary formats).
// Spec: https://go.dev/ref/spec#Numeric_types.
//
//   - Bits32 — `float32`  (IEEE-754 binary32 single-precision)
//   - Bits64 — `float64`  (IEEE-754 binary64 double-precision; the
//                          Go idiomatic default float width)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). f16 / f128 stabilization in a future Go
// Specification revision would be an edit to THIS declaration under
// the L-2 pin discipline.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every float-scalar consumer reads the
//      width to size representations (emission of `float32` vs
//      `float64`; cost-realization for floating-point ops); shared
//      classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ bits: Int }` proxy (32/64)
//      replaces a closed 2-valued exhaustive sum with an unbounded
//      Int — and floating-point widths are NOT free Int values;
//      they are the IEEE-754-binary-format-admitted subset Go
//      stabilizes (P2 illegal-states-unrepresentable; same shape
//      as SignedIntWidth / UnsignedIntWidth and rust.dag
//      RustFloatWidth).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the two widths
//      structurally inhabit the SAME algebra (std/algebra.dag
//      `ApproximateField` per the rounding-aware framing —
//      INVARIANTS:43, std/float.dag scaffold's anchor); they do
//      not partition into distinct algebraic structures. No richer
//      std/ source to collapse the enum INTO.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single typed float has EXACTLY ONE
//      width; mutually-exclusive alternatives. (Go does not admit
//      `float32 ⊓ float64` as a hybrid type.)
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the two widths are the Go-
//      stable-IEEE-754-binary-format set; they are NOT one generic
//      `F<X>` / projection over a separately-declared source set
//      (Practice 7). A richer IEEE-754 source (the full binary16/
//      32/64/128 set) is the spec's universe, but Go admits only
//      the binary32/64 subset stably — the choice of "which widths
//      Go stabilizes" is the partition discriminant, not a
//      parameterization of a richer source set.
// Terminal: the closed two-way Go stable-float-width classifier.
type GoFloatWidth
  = Bits32
  | Bits64


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// GoComplexWidth — the closed Go complex-number bit-width classifier.
// The Go Specification §Numeric_types admits exactly two complex
// widths: `complex64` (two `float32` components) and `complex128`
// (two `float64` components).
// Spec: https://go.dev/ref/spec#Numeric_types.
//
//   - Bits64  — `complex64`   (real and imaginary components are
//                              each IEEE-754 binary32; total 64
//                              bits of representation)
//   - Bits128 — `complex128`  (real and imaginary components are
//                              each IEEE-754 binary64; total 128
//                              bits of representation)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). Adding a complex-of-float16 or complex-
// of-float128 width would be an edit to THIS declaration under the
// L-2 pin discipline (Go has not stabilized these).
//
// SPEC FAITHFULNESS NOTE (INVARIANTS P1). Go's complex types are
// PRIMITIVE per spec §Numeric_types (the Specification lists them
// alongside the integer and float types in the predeclared types
// list — they are not composite types built from `(float, float)`
// at the language layer; the spec treats them as their own typed
// scalar values). They therefore appear as a kind partition member
// in GoScalarKind below, not as a composite-type construction. This
// is a structural Go-vs-Rust difference — Rust has no primitive
// complex type (complex arithmetic lives in libraries like
// `num-complex`, which are programs per L-2, NOT primitives).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every complex-scalar consumer reads
//      the width to size representations (emission of `complex64`
//      vs `complex128`; cost-realization for complex ops which
//      decompose into per-component float ops); shared classifier,
//      not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ component_bits: Int }` proxy
//      (32 → complex64, 64 → complex128) replaces a closed 2-valued
//      exhaustive sum with an unbounded Int (the same P2 illegal-
//      states-unrepresentable shape). The enum IS the faithful
//      form.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: complex64 / complex128
//      inhabit the SAME structure (the rounding-aware complex-
//      number analog; an ApproximateField over a Conj of two same-
//      width floats); they do not partition into distinct
//      algebraic structures. The inhabitance ride D2 per "Owned
//      ELSEWHERE".
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single typed complex has EXACTLY ONE
//      width; mutually-exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — PARTIALLY: each complex width
//      structurally projects from the corresponding GoFloatWidth
//      (complex64 → Bits32, complex128 → Bits64) — `F<X> =
//      complex-of-float-width-X`. However, this projection-uniformity
//      is INCIDENTAL: the spec does not define complex types as a
//      generic family parameterized over the float widths; they are
//      enumerated as two named primitive types. Pointing this enum
//      AT GoFloatWidth would parallel-represent the spec's
//      enumeration. The carrier stays a closed 2-way enum; the
//      projection is observed in commentary, not encoded as a
//      Practice 7 collapse.
// Terminal: the closed two-way Go stable-complex-width classifier.
type GoComplexWidth
  = Bits64
  | Bits128


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// GoScalarKind — the closed Go scalar-type kind classifier. The Go
// Specification §Types partitions the predeclared primitive types
// into exactly five kinds, per §Numeric_types (the Int / Float /
// Complex bifurcation), §Boolean_types, and §String_types.
// Spec: https://go.dev/ref/spec#Types.
//
//   - Int      — integer scalars (Spec §Numeric_types — both signed
//                and unsigned, all widths — see SignedIntWidth /
//                UnsignedIntWidth)
//   - Float    — floating-point scalars (Spec §Numeric_types —
//                float32 / float64 stable — see GoFloatWidth)
//   - Complex  — complex-number scalars (Spec §Numeric_types —
//                complex64 / complex128 stable — see GoComplexWidth;
//                a primitive scalar kind in Go, structurally distinct
//                from Rust which has no primitive complex)
//   - Bool     — the `bool` two-valued type (Spec §Boolean_types;
//                classical-logic-inhabiting per std/logic.dag)
//   - String   — the `string` immutable byte-sequence type (Spec
//                §String_types; an immutable sequence of bytes — Go
//                strings are NOT guaranteed UTF-8 by the spec,
//                though idiomatic Go strings are UTF-8 and the
//                language has explicit `rune` / `[]byte` conversion
//                surfaces. Inhabits FreeMonoid<Byte> per "Owned
//                ELSEWHERE" — the UTF-8 interpretation is a separate
//                lens-read).
//
// Aliases (`byte` for `uint8`, `rune` for `int32`) are DELIBERATELY
// NOT in this partition: the Go Specification §Numeric_types defines
// them as aliases — they are the SAME types as their targets, not
// new kinds. Per P2 single-authority, alias names live in the
// boundary intern table (resolved at parse time); kernel/lens code
// reads the underlying `UnsignedIntScalar { width: UnsignedBits8 }`
// (for `byte` ≡ `uint8`) or `SignedIntScalar { width: SignedBits32 }`
// (for `rune` ≡ `int32`). The alias spelling never appears in this
// partition.
//
// `error` (Go's standard error interface) is DELIBERATELY NOT in
// this partition: it is an INTERFACE TYPE, not a primitive scalar
// (Spec §Errors). Composite types are out-of-scope per the header
// SCOPE NEGATIVE.
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A sixth scalar kind is an edit to THIS
// declaration under the L-2 pin discipline. The kind enum is the
// meta-classifier; the typed scalar values themselves are the
// `GoScalar` Disj below (which carries the kind × width
// admissibility as structural variant payloads).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every Go-typed value consumer
//      branches on the scalar kind to dispatch (which algebra
//      applies, which width is meaningful, which emission syntax
//      to use); shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ name: Symbol }` proxy reopens
//      the readable-string heuristic surface K-1 forecloses (a
//      `match name { "int32" => … }` smell, exactly the v3
//      regression the K-1 opacity discipline structurally
//      prevents). The enum IS the faithful form.
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: each kind grounds in a std/algebra
//      structure (Int → OrderedRing for SignedIntScalar / Semiring
//      for UnsignedIntScalar, Float
//      → ApproximateField, Complex → ApproximateField over a Conj
//      of two same-width floats, Bool → BooleanAlgebra, String →
//      FreeMonoid<Byte> per the spec's byte-sequence semantics).
//      That grounding is the INHABITANCE walk owned by the std/
//      scalar files per P2 (rides D2 + T-3 wave A3). The kind enum
//      itself stays a closed classifier; the "kind → algebra" map
//      IS the future inhabitance walk, not a richer std/ source to
//      collapse this enum INTO. Same shape as ptx.dag
//      RegisterScalarKind and rust.dag RustScalarKind.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single Go scalar value has EXACTLY
//      ONE kind; mutually-exclusive alternatives. (Kind × Width is
//      the per-variant width carrier on GoScalar's SignedIntScalar /
//      UnsignedIntScalar / FloatScalar / ComplexScalar variants —
//      that IS the dimensional model for the width-bearing kinds.
//      The Signed/Unsigned partition is structurally split across
//      two integer variants rather than carried as a shared
//      GoIntKind axis, because the Cartesian product is ASYMMETRIC
//      — see GoScalar header.)
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the five kinds are the Go-
//      Specification primitive-type partition; they are NOT one
//      generic `F<X>` / projection over a separately-declared
//      source set (Practice 7). The kinds ground in STRUCTURALLY
//      HETEROGENEOUS algebraic structures (OrderedRing/Semiring/
//      ApproximateField/ApproximateField-over-Conj-of-floats/
//      BooleanAlgebra/FreeMonoid — five distinct std/algebra
//      structures). The partition is the spec's irreducible
//      primitive-kind distinction, not a copy of any enumerable
//      source.
// Terminal: the closed five-way Go primitive-scalar-kind classifier
// for the pinned Go Specification.
type GoScalarKind
  = Int
  | Float
  | Complex
  | Bool
  | String


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// GoScalar — a typed Go primitive-scalar value. Disj sum with NAMED-
// FIELD VARIANT PAYLOADS so that ALL admissibility constraints are
// STRUCTURALLY ENFORCED, not "rejected by a check":
//   * Bool / String carry no width payload — "Bool with width=Bits32"
//     is unrepresentable.
//   * Signed and unsigned integers are SEPARATE variants carrying
//     SEPARATE width sums — "Signed × PointerSized" (which has no
//     Go analogue — `uintptr` is unsigned-only per spec
//     §Numeric_types) is unrepresentable. This is the cursor/composer-
//     2 BLOCKING-review fix on PR #3182 2026-05-16: the prior unified
//     `IntScalar { kind: GoIntKind, width: GoIntWidth }` Cartesian-
//     product carrier admitted the missing Signed × PointerSized
//     cell — INVARIANTS P2 illegal-states-unrepresentable anti-pattern.
//     The dissolution splits widths per signedness; the rust.dag
//     #3174 unified-IntScalar pattern works for Rust because Rust's
//     Cartesian product is COMPLETE (isize/usize are symmetric); Go's
//     is asymmetric and requires the per-signedness split. Per-language
//     dissolution shape, same INVARIANTS P2 principle.
// Spec: https://go.dev/ref/spec#Types.
//
//   - SignedIntScalar    — { width: SignedIntWidth }
//                          e.g. SignedIntScalar { width: SignedBits32 }
//                          is Go's `int32`; SignedIntScalar { width:
//                          SignedPlatformInt } is `int`. There is no
//                          SignedPointerSized variant — Go has no
//                          signed-pointer-sized type.
//   - UnsignedIntScalar  — { width: UnsignedIntWidth }
//                          e.g. UnsignedIntScalar { width: UnsignedBits32 }
//                          is Go's `uint32`; UnsignedIntScalar { width:
//                          UnsignedPointerSized } is `uintptr`.
//   - FloatScalar        — { width: GoFloatWidth }
//                          e.g. FloatScalar { width: Bits64 } is `float64`.
//   - ComplexScalar      — { width: GoComplexWidth }
//                          e.g. ComplexScalar { width: Bits128 } is
//                          `complex128`; the COMPONENT widths
//                          (binary32 for complex64, binary64 for
//                          complex128) are DERIVED from the complex
//                          width per spec §Numeric_types — not a
//                          separate payload.
//   - BoolScalar         — Go's `bool` (the two-valued type — there
//                          is no width variant).
//   - StringScalar       — Go's `string` (immutable byte sequence;
//                          Spec §String_types. No width variant; the
//                          byte-sequence length is a value-level fact,
//                          not a kind-level discriminator).
//
// The structural argument for the Disj-with-heterogeneous-payloads
// (vs ptx's Conj-record `RegisterScalar { kind, width, state_space }`):
// in PTX every typed register has a (kind, width, state_space), so
// the three-coordinate Conj is the natural form. In Go the six scalar
// variants have DIFFERENT structural shapes — Signed/Unsigned integers
// carry their own per-signedness width sum, Float / Complex carry a
// single width, Bool / String have nothing beyond the variant tag. A
// Conj record `{ kind, width? }` would force a width Option for
// Bool/String AND would re-admit the Signed × PointerSized illegal
// state the per-signedness split exists to forbid — exactly the M4 /
// Practice-2/6 / P2 pattern those disciplines structurally forbid.
// The Disj-with-heterogeneous-payloads + per-signedness-width split
// is the dissolved form: each variant carries EXACTLY the structural
// data its kind admits, and no illegal kind×width combination is
// constructible.
//
// SEAM (where D2 inhabitance instance-values plug in). The D2a three-
// thin-facts resolver form is operator-ratified (DECISIONS.md D2 row;
// D2 block below). When upstream std/ carriers land (T-3 Wave-A3), the
// per-scalar alias + grounding + operation-semantics rows amend into
// this file with these typed variants as their carriers — SignedIntScalar
// { width: SignedBits32 } is the carrier surface for Go's `int32`
// alias-identity into `Int32` (through std/integer.dag — NOT a parallel-
// substrate OrderedRing row); UnsignedIntScalar { width:
// UnsignedPointerSized } is the carrier for `uintptr`; FloatScalar {
// width: Bits32 } is the carrier for `float32` → ApproximateField;
// ComplexScalar { width: Bits128 } is the carrier for `complex128` →
// ApproximateField over a Conj of two binary64 floats; StringScalar is
// the carrier for `string` → FreeMonoid<Byte>. The seam itself is the
// typed variant; the D2a inhabitance rows are the deferred additions.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every Go-typed value consumer
//      (literal parsing, expression typing, emission to source,
//      cost-realization, eventual inhabitance dispatch) reads the
//      scalar's variant + width-if-applicable through this carrier;
//      shared, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS (twice): (a) collapsing to a uniform
//      `{ kind: GoScalarKind, width: Option<…> }` reintroduces the
//      illegal state "Bool with width=Bits32" the heterogeneous-
//      payload design exists to forbid. (b) collapsing the integer
//      variants back to a unified `IntScalar { kind: GoIntKind,
//      width: GoIntWidth }` reintroduces "Signed × PointerSized" —
//      the Cartesian-product anti-pattern this PR specifically
//      dissolves. The six-variant Disj IS the dissolved form
//      (P2 + Practice 2/6 + M4).
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: each variant grounds in a std/
//      algebra or cardinality structure (SignedIntScalar →
//      OrderedRing; UnsignedIntScalar → Semiring; FloatScalar →
//      ApproximateField; ComplexScalar → ApproximateField over a
//      Conj of two same-width floats; BoolScalar → BooleanAlgebra;
//      StringScalar → FreeMonoid<Byte> per the spec's byte-sequence
//      semantics). That grounding IS the inhabitance walk owned
//      ELSEWHERE per P2; the Disj structure here is the closed
//      surface, not a collapsing target.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single Go scalar value IS EXACTLY ONE
//      variant of this Disj; mutually-exclusive alternatives. The
//      shapes differ across variants (Signed/Unsigned integer
//      variants carry their own per-signedness width sum; Float /
//      Complex carry one width; Bool / String carry no payload) —
//      they cannot be coordinates of a single record without the
//      illegal-state smells.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the six variants are NOT one
//      generic `F<X>` / projection over a separately-declared
//      source set (Practice 7). The Disj has heterogeneous
//      structural shapes (the per-signedness width split makes
//      SignedIntScalar and UnsignedIntScalar carry STRUCTURALLY
//      DIFFERENT width sums — SignedIntWidth has 5 variants,
//      UnsignedIntWidth has 6 — they cannot be projected over a
//      single source width set, AND the algebraic groundings span
//      four distinct std/algebra structures plus BooleanAlgebra
//      plus FreeMonoid<Byte>). The Disj-with-heterogeneous-payloads
//      is the irreducible faithful form for Go's asymmetric
//      integer partition.
// Terminal: the closed six-variant Go primitive-scalar typed-value
// sum; illegal kind×width combinations (Bool×width, Signed×Pointer-
// Sized) are unrepresentable by construction; composite types are
// scope-negative for this slice.
type GoScalar
  = SignedIntScalar { width: SignedIntWidth }
  | UnsignedIntScalar { width: UnsignedIntWidth }
  | FloatScalar { width: GoFloatWidth }
  | ComplexScalar { width: GoComplexWidth }
  | BoolScalar
  | StringScalar


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// GoChannelDirection — the closed Go channel-direction classifier.
// The Go Specification §Channel_types admits exactly three channel
// directions (the send-vs-receive-vs-both partition the type system
// enforces at the channel-type level).
// Spec: https://go.dev/ref/spec#Channel_types.
//
//   - SendRecv  — `chan T`   bidirectional channel; admits both
//                            sends (`ch <- v`) and receives (`<-ch`)
//   - SendOnly  — `chan<- T` send-only channel; admits only sends;
//                            using `<-ch` on a SendOnly channel is
//                            a static type error
//   - RecvOnly  — `<-chan T` receive-only channel; admits only
//                            receives; using `ch <- v` on a RecvOnly
//                            channel is a static type error
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A fourth direction is an edit to THIS
// declaration routed to operator ratification under the L-2 pin
// discipline.
//
// IN-B SEAM (effects intrinsic to the type signature, THESIS:401).
// The GoChannel Conj record below ATTACHES this direction to an
// opaque element-type-reference Symbol, making the channel a
// structural Conj field on a typed value (the same shape as ptx.dag's
// `state_space: StateSpace` on RegisterScalar and rust.dag's
// RustReference). The COORDINATION / EFFECT LENS reads this field
// from typed signatures; the channel-direction-vs-operation legality
// rules are a STRUCTURAL property of the signature, not a parallel
// channel-discipline taxonomy. This is the Go-specific instantiation
// of the IN-B principle "effects are intrinsic to the type signature",
// applied to the goroutine/channel concurrency model.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every channel-bearing-typed-value
//      consumer (the coordination lens, the emission of `chan T` /
//      `chan<- T` / `<-chan T`, the per-direction operation-
//      legality check) reads the direction tag uniformly; shared
//      classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ can_send: Bool, can_recv:
//      Bool }` proxy replaces a closed 3-valued exhaustive sum
//      (where the spec excludes the (false, false) "neither" case)
//      with an unbounded 4-state record that ratifies the illegal
//      "neither send nor receive" state — exactly the P2 illegal-
//      states-unrepresentable anti-shape. The enum IS the faithful
//      form.
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: the three directions form a
//      DEGENERATE-LATTICE-LIKE shape (SendRecv subsumes both
//      SendOnly and RecvOnly; SendOnly and RecvOnly are
//      incomparable siblings — `SendRecv ⊒ SendOnly` and `SendRecv
//      ⊒ RecvOnly`). But the subsumption is DERIVED FROM the closed
//      enum (it is a property of "which directions a typed channel
//      value can be assigned to"), not a richer source — pointing
//      to a `BoundedLattice<GoChannelDirection>` would parallel-
//      represent the direction taxonomy the order is defined over
//      (algebra.dag U1 engine-debt smell, same argument as ptx.dag
//      BarrierScope's ledger and rust.dag RustVisibility's ledger).
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single channel-typed value has
//      EXACTLY ONE direction; mutually-exclusive alternatives.
//      (The assignment-direction subsumption — `chan T` is
//      assignable to `chan<- T` — is a TYPE-LEVEL conversion, not
//      a same-value-with-multiple-directions state.)
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: SendRecv / SendOnly /
//      RecvOnly are the Go Specification's channel-direction
//      partition; they are NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection
//      over a separately-declared source set (Practice 7). The
//      directions correspond to STRUCTURALLY DISTINCT operation-
//      legality disciplines (admits-send-and-receive vs admits-
//      send-only vs admits-receive-only) — they are not
//      projection-uniform over any richer source.
// Terminal: the closed three-way Go channel-direction partition.
type GoChannelDirection
  = SendRecv
  | SendOnly
  | RecvOnly


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// GoExportLocus — the closed Go export-eligible declaration-locus
// classifier. The Go Specification §Exported_identifiers makes export-
// status a TWO-CONDITION property: an identifier is exported iff BOTH
// (a) the first character is a Unicode upper-case letter (Lu), AND
// (b) the identifier is declared in the package block OR is a field
// name OR is a method name. Locus (b) is what this carrier closes —
// a function-local declaration with an upper-case first letter is
// NOT exported per the spec, because it fails condition (b).
// Spec: https://go.dev/ref/spec#Exported_identifiers.
//
// The carrier exists to make condition (b) structural rather than
// producer-discipline. Without it, GoExportStatus could be attached
// to any identifier (function-local included), and "exported local
// uppercase identifier" — which is a spec-impossibility — would be
// representable. Adding the locus tag and coupling it to
// GoExportStatus's variants makes the impossible state
// unrepresentable, per INVARIANTS P1 modeling-faithfulness +
// modeling-discipline.md Practice 2 illegal-states-unrepresentable
// (cursor/composer-2 BLOCKING review on PR #3182 2026-05-16: the
// prior binary-only GoExportStatus admitted exported-local-uppercase
// identifiers — a violation of P1's spec-partition discipline).
//
//   - PackageBlock      — a declaration directly in the package
//                         block (top-level `var`, `const`, `type`,
//                         `func`). Per spec the most common eligible
//                         locus.
//   - StructField       — a field name in a struct type declaration.
//                         Per spec §Struct_types the field name's
//                         case-derived export-status governs cross-
//                         package access to the field.
//   - MethodName        — a method name. Per spec the "method name"
//                         locus class covers BOTH (a) method names
//                         declared inside an interface type (`type
//                         X interface { Method() }` per §Interface_
//                         types) AND (b) concrete-type method
//                         declarations (`func (recv T) Method() {…}`
//                         per §Method_declarations). The spec
//                         enumerates these uniformly under "method
//                         name" — both flavors carry the case-
//                         derived export-status that governs cross-
//                         package access to the method. (Broadened
//                         2026-05-16 per cursor BLOCKING finding on
//                         PR #3182: the prior InterfaceMethod
//                         variant excluded concrete methods like
//                         `func (T) Lock()`, leaving them with no
//                         representable locus — INVARIANTS P1/P2
//                         violation.)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). The Go Specification names exactly these
// three eligible loci for export-status. A fourth would be a future
// language-version change requiring edit under the L-2 pin discipline.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every export-status consumer (the
//      cross-package-name-resolution walk, the public-API-surface
//      synthesis) reads the locus uniformly to know whether the
//      identifier is actually export-eligible per spec; shared
//      classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ name: Symbol }` proxy reopens
//      the readable-string heuristic surface K-1 forecloses (a
//      `match name { "package_block" => … }` smell); a `{ depth:
//      Int }` proxy replaces a closed 3-valued exhaustive sum with
//      an unbounded Int. The enum IS the faithful form.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the three loci are
//      the Go Specification's export-eligible-declaration partition;
//      they do not trace to a richer std/ algebraic structure (one
//      could attempt a containment partial-order — package-block
//      contains type declarations contain fields/methods — but
//      that ordering is derived FROM the closed enum, not a richer
//      source).
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single export-eligible declaration is
//      in EXACTLY ONE locus (a struct field is not a method name; a
//      package-block declaration is not a field name); mutually-
//      exclusive alternatives. (Concrete methods and interface
//      methods both unify under MethodName per spec §Exported_
//      identifiers, sharing one locus class.)
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the three loci are
//      structurally distinct declaration kinds (package-level
//      binding / member of a struct type / method name — covering
//      both interface and concrete method declarations) — NOT one
//      generic `F<X>` / projection over a separately-declared
//      source set (Practice 7). The Go-Spec enumeration is the
//      irreducible partition.
// Terminal: the closed three-way Go export-eligible-locus classifier.
type GoExportLocus
  = PackageBlock
  | StructField
  | MethodName


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// GoExportStatus — the closed Go identifier-visibility classifier.
// The Go Specification §Exported_identifiers defines visibility as a
// TWO-CONDITION property: an identifier is exported iff BOTH (a) the
// first character is a Unicode upper-case letter (Lu) AND (b) the
// identifier is declared in the package block or is a field name or
// method name. Function-local identifiers — even uppercase ones —
// are NOT exported per the spec. The model couples both conditions
// structurally: a GoExportStatus carrier exists ONLY for identifiers
// in eligible loci (see GoExportLocus above), and its variant carries
// the locus payload. Function-local declarations DO NOT receive a
// GoExportStatus tag at all — the carrier itself represents the
// eligible-locus partition (cursor/composer-2 BLOCKING fix on
// PR #3182 2026-05-16: the prior binary-only carrier admitted
// "exported function-local uppercase identifier" — a spec-
// impossibility now structurally unrepresentable).
// Spec: https://go.dev/ref/spec#Exported_identifiers.
//
//   - Exported    — { locus: GoExportLocus }
//                   first letter is upper-case Unicode letter (Lu)
//                   AND identifier is in an export-eligible locus.
//                   Per spec: "An identifier is exported if both:
//                   (1) the first character of the identifier's name
//                   is a Unicode upper case letter (Unicode class
//                   'Lu'); and (2) the identifier is declared in the
//                   package block or it is a field name or method
//                   name." Both conditions structurally required by
//                   construction.
//   - Unexported  — { locus: GoExportLocus }
//                   first letter is NOT upper-case (lower-case Lu,
//                   digit, underscore) AND identifier is in an
//                   export-eligible locus. A package-block lower-case
//                   identifier, a lower-case struct-field name, etc.
//
// FUNCTION-LOCAL DECLARATIONS DO NOT CARRY GoExportStatus. A
// `func main() { var X int }` — the local `X` — is not export-
// eligible per spec; emitting a GoExportStatus tag on it would
// violate the spec partition. The parser MUST NOT attach this carrier
// to local-block identifiers. The structural enforcement: the
// carrier's mere PRESENCE on a declaration is the spec's condition
// (b); a declaration that fails (b) has NO GoExportStatus.
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). Go has no intermediate visibility scopes;
// the binary upper-case-or-not partition is the entire visibility
// taxonomy per spec. A third scope would be a future language-
// version change requiring edit under the L-2 pin discipline.
//
// K-1 BOUNDARY NOTE. The export-status of a Go identifier is a
// FUNCTION of its spelling (the first character's Unicode category)
// AND its declaration locus. Per K-1 (node.dag:70-86) `Symbol` is
// OPAQUE: kernel/lens code cannot read a Symbol's spelling. Both
// conditions are therefore resolved AT THE PARSER BOUNDARY (where
// the spelling IS readable AND the AST locus is known) and ATTACHED
// to eligible-locus declarations as a structural tag. Kernel/lens
// code reads `export_status: GoExportStatus`, never the underlying
// Symbol spelling and never re-walks the locus.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every Go-item consumer (emission of
//      the identifier preserving case, the cross-package-name-
//      resolution walk, the public-API-surface synthesis for
//      documentation / coordination contracts) reads the export
//      status uniformly; shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ exported: Bool, locus:
//      GoExportLocus }` proxy is structurally equivalent on the
//      type-level but loses the case-derived semantic distinction
//      (Exported vs Unexported is the Spec's terminology) and the
//      named-enum is the M4-faithful form. Promoting to a unified
//      `{ exported: Bool }` proxy (dropping locus) reintroduces the
//      "exported function-local uppercase" illegal state — the
//      INVARIANTS P2 anti-pattern this PR's BLOCKING-fix dissolves.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the two statuses are
//      the Go Specification's export partition; they do not trace
//      to a richer std/ algebraic structure (one could attempt a
//      `BoundedLattice<ExportStatus>` with Exported = top, but
//      that would be a parallel representation of the import-
//      visibility containment, a property derived FROM the two
//      tags).
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single export-eligible Go identifier
//      has EXACTLY ONE export status (the first-letter-case test
//      is exhaustive and mutually exclusive over eligible-locus
//      identifiers); mutually-exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — PARTIALLY: both variants are
//      structurally identical except for the case-derived axis
//      (each carries `{ locus: GoExportLocus }`), so this enum IS
//      projection-uniform over the locus-bearing record shape.
//      However, the projection-source is GoExportLocus itself
//      (already declared as its own closed Disj above), and
//      collapsing Exported/Unexported into a single record
//      `{ exported: Bool, locus: GoExportLocus }` loses the Spec's
//      named partition (Spec §Exported_identifiers names "exported"
//      explicitly; the Unexported partition is its negation but
//      has its own consumer-side semantics). Keeping the named
//      two-variant Disj preserves the Spec partition's terminology
//      while the locus carrier is the projection-uniform component.
// Terminal: the closed two-way Go identifier-visibility partition
// over export-eligible loci; first-letter case is the discriminator;
// declaration locus is the carrier payload coupling both spec
// conditions structurally.
type GoExportStatus
  = Exported { locus: GoExportLocus }
  | Unexported { locus: GoExportLocus }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// GoChannel — a typed Go channel: direction × element-type identity.
// Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger — only coproducts dissolve).
// Spec: https://go.dev/ref/spec#Channel_types.
//
//   - direction          which channel direction (SendRecv /
//                        SendOnly / RecvOnly — the partition the
//                        type system enforces at the channel-type
//                        level)
//   - element_type_ref   the channel's element type, as an opaque
//                        K-1 Symbol referring to a type declaration
//                        resolved through the namespace (per
//                        node.dag's name-reference idiom — A1).
//                        Inline / unnamed element types (`chan
//                        struct{}`, `chan func()`) are out-of-scope
//                        for this slice — the bundled T-4
//                        LanguageModel grammar productions resolve
//                        type expressions; at this vocabulary
//                        layer, the channel's element is named via
//                        Symbol just as Rust's lifetime is named
//                        via Symbol (rust.dag #3174 precedent).
//
// IN-B / structural-fact discipline (per the header STRUCTURAL
// FINDING). The GoChannel Conj record IS the structural surface of
// the IN-B claim "Go coordination is intrinsic to the type
// signature, NOT an annotation layer" (THESIS:401, scaffold header
// IN-B framing applied to Go's channel-direction locus). Channel
// I/O through a typed channel is a Transform whose operand type
// CARRIES this record in its signature; the coordination / effect
// lens READS the (direction, element_type_ref) coordinates directly
// from the typed signature and validates well-typedness as a
// structural property — there is no separate "channel-discipline
// annotation" layer. Same shape as rust.dag's RustReference and
// ptx.dag's RegisterScalar.state_space.
type GoChannel {
  direction: GoChannelDirection
  element_type_ref: Symbol
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// GoCost — the per-target cost-realization carrier shape for the Go
// `LanguageModel`. Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
// This declares the SHAPE the U1 spine reads when computing Go-side
// realization cost — both components per the scaffold header's "cost
// realization for Go" claim (preserved as a goal; the carrier shape
// lands now, the cost INSTANCE-VALUES land downstream per "Owned
// ELSEWHERE"):
//
//   - instruction_cost   per-operation throughput cost (the U1 per-
//                        target-op cost projection — the `map` side
//                        of `Homomorphism<…, …, GoScalar>` carries
//                        this; cost is read FROM the map's structure,
//                        never stored as a parallel field per
//                        algebra.dag's U1 no-engine discipline)
//   - allocation_cost    per-allocation cost — in Go this carries
//                        BOTH the heap-allocation throughput AND the
//                        GC-pressure contribution (Go's escape-
//                        analyzer determines whether a value is
//                        stack- or heap-allocated; heap allocations
//                        contribute to GC pressure measured by the
//                        runtime). The per-target shape that the
//                        cost lens T-12 reads when synthesizing a
//                        Go realization's total cost. The GC's
//                        internal model (mark-sweep generations,
//                        write barriers) is per-runtime-
//                        implementation detail, not encoded here —
//                        only the structural "this operation
//                        allocates" fact composes through this
//                        carrier.
//
// 🟡 SCAFFOLD (same bridge as std/diagnostic.dag's `Extent.ByteRange`,
// ptx.dag's `Dim3` / `PtxCost`, and rust.dag's `RustCost`). With only
// kernel-ambient `Int` available at the T-4 phase, `{ instruction_cost:
// Int, allocation_cost: Int }` STRUCTURALLY ADMITS illegal states
// (negative costs; nonsense magnitudes). modeling-discipline.md §2
// (illegal-states-unrepresentable) forbids admitting them; no
// bounded-Cardinality refinement substrate exists yet to exclude
// them. It is a TRACKED scaffold, declared with the three bridge
// properties (the precedent diagnostic.dag established 2026-05-15):
//   (a) scaffold doc — THIS note; the carrier is provisional, not
//       the terminal refined-positive shape.
//   (b) bounds on use — a producer MUST construct only non-negative
//       costs; until the substrate enforces it the invariant is
//       producer-side; a GoCost with negative components is a
//       producer bug, not a legal value.
//   (c) dissolution trigger — when std/cardinality.dag's refinement
//       substrate lands (T-3, P4 decidability), `instruction_cost`
//       and `allocation_cost` become refined bounded-non-negative-
//       Int axes, and this scaffold dissolves.
//
// SEAM (where T-12 cost-lens reads). When lens/cost.dag (T-12, U2)
// lands, its `SymbolicCost` lattice carrier composes over the per-
// target projections; the Go projection is read from this Conj
// record's components per the U2 "complexity consumes cost, never
// re-derives" discipline. Pre-T-12, GoCost is the declared SHAPE the
// projection plugs into; concrete cost instance-values per operation
// (e.g. `Add<Int>` costs `GoCost { instruction_cost: 1,
// allocation_cost: 0 }`) ride T-12 + D2 per "Owned ELSEWHERE."
type GoCost {
  instruction_cost: Int
  allocation_cost: Int
}


// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// D2 RESOLVER SHAPE — per-primitive three-thin-facts declarations.
//
// Per the operator-ratified DECISIONS.md D2 row (2026-05-16, encoded
// via PR #3195 merge at 44a37ad46), a language extdeps file is a
// RESOLVER for the abstract std/ carriers — the
// `infra(abstract) → gcloud/aws(concrete)` pattern. Per primitive,
// THREE thin declarations:
//
//   (1) alias-identity to the std/ carrier (`type GoInt32 = Int32`)
//       — the machine-readable-inhabitance form (`List<T> =
//       FreeMonoid<T>` precedent); the algebra inhabitance flows
//       through the alias, so "see through the labels" is literal
//       Node identity.
//   (2) grounding map (surface spelling — `int32`).
//   (3) operation-semantics carriers (OverflowDisposition,
//       NaN/Inf handling) — genuine per-language facts, siblings
//       to the alias.
//
// Per the D2 row: a per-language `data go_<prim>_ring: OrderedRing<
// GoPrim, …>` inhabitance is the parallel-substrate INVARIANTS P1:42
// forbids (`numeric_aliases_align_to_refinements`) — superseded.
// Structural mirror of rust.dag #3174 @0328bee951962382aa60143bf563c7b9eb39d215
// (verified canonical rust head for the D2 reshape).
//
// Per the D2 row's D2.2: coercion = the U1 homomorphism realized by
// the generic B2-OMNI emit fold reading the resolver data. NOT a
// hand table, NOT a per-language emit lens (B2-OMNI/B4: one generic
// emit, target-specificity is declarative T-11 data).
//
// Per the D2 row's D2.3: compound/generic coercion composes for
// FREE via the Node catamorphism (A1's single recursive type;
// `List<Int32>` = `Instantiation` recursed); no per-instantiation
// rows. Not blocked on the Functor hierarchy.
//
// Per the D2 row's D2.4: round-trip guarantee is structural-on-F
// per C5-1; TestClaims document it, never establish it.
//
// Per the D2 row's D2.5: overflow/UB/arbitrary-precision are
// DISTINCT std/ carriers selected by the alias target + the
// per-language OverflowDisposition carrier, never a re-declared
// semantics.
//
// Per the D2 row's scope guard: D2 covers per-primitive inhabitance
// form ONLY. It does NOT decide LanguageModel-grammar facts — those
// ride C5-3 / B2-OMNI / B4 per their own cascade. Grammar-slice
// workers must not import D2 reasoning into them.
//
// CURRENT-MAIN GROUNDING STATE (snapshot 2026-05-16 post-#3195 merge):
//   - std/logic.dag: `Bool` LANDED (#3166) → GoBool alias-identity
//     + grounding-map land NOW (this block).
//   - std/nat.dag: `Nat` LANDED — Go has no arbitrary-precision Nat
//     primitive (every Go numeric primitive is bounded or IEEE/complex
//     shaped); no direct alias.
//   - std/machine.dag: `Word*` / `MachineWidth` / `PointerWidth`
//     LANDED — machine-width substrate for eventual `int` / `uint` /
//     `uintptr` platform widths; they compose with std/integer.dag's
//     eventual bounded ladders; they don't standalone-alias from
//     go.dag.
//   - std/text.dag: `Char = Nat` and `String = FreeMonoid<Char>`
//     LANDED — Go `string` is a byte sequence (not necessarily valid
//     UTF-8 per spec); alias-identity to `String` is a REFINEMENT
//     question parallel to rust.dag's `str` deferral — DEFERRED pending
//     refined std/text.dag carriers OR a per-language refinement-
//     disposition carrier (D2 scope-guard ambiguity).
//   - std/integer.dag: NOT YET FILLED (scaffold only). Integer aliases
//     (GoInt8 = Int8 through GoInt64 = Int64 / GoInt for `int` /
//     GoUint8..GoUint64 / GoUint / GoUintptr) DEFERRED pending T-3
//     Wave-A3.
//   - std/float.dag: NOT YET FILLED. Float aliases (GoFloat32 =
//     Float32 / GoFloat64 = Float64) + complex carrier questions
//     DEFERRED pending T-3 Wave-A3.
//   - Never carrier: std/cardinality.dag has Multiplicity but no
//     explicit empty-cardinality / Never type. GoNever alias DEFERRED
