// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// GroundingMap<IRCarrier> — the per-primitive surface-spelling
// carrier. Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
// Per the D2 row D2a(2): a "grounding map (surface spelling)".
// Each Go primitive's alias-identity declaration is paired with a
// `data <name>_grounding: GroundingMap<<AliasName>> = GroundingMap
// { spelling: "<source-token>" }` row that names the Go source token
// for emit / ingest. The spelling is a `String` boundary value (the
// canonical surface text); it lives at the source-syntax boundary,
// not in kernel/lens code.
//
// Spec: https://go.dev/ref/spec#Types (pinned go1.26 — per this file's
//       Anchor header; per-primitive spec anchors land on the alias-
//       identity rows in the deferred ledger below.)
type GroundingMap<IRCarrier> {
  spelling: String
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// OverflowAction — the closed disposition carrier **shape** shared
// with rust.dag for homogeneous downstream typing (rust.dag @0328bee9
// precedent). Go 1.26 differs materially from Rust: well-typed built-
// in `+ - * /` on integer **types** uses two's-complement modular
// truncation on overflow for BOTH signed and unsigned widths — there
// is **no** Rust-style "debug panics / release wraps" split for these
// operators (Go spec §Integer_overflow, pinned go1.26).
//
//   - PanicOnOverflow      — **not** the Go 1.26 default for ordinary
//                            built-in integer `+ - * /`; retained as the
//                            ratified coproduct's second member mirroring
//                            rust.dag's closed Disj (C1 stop-signal if a
//                            future Go edition introduces a panic default
//                            — would be an L-2-visible edit here).
//   - TwoComplementWrap    — the Go 1.26 built-in default for these ops
//                            on typed integer widths.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Same five-pattern ledger shape as rust.dag's
// `OverflowAction` — the per-language **facts** differ; the resolver
// carrier family stays isomorphic at the type level.
//
// Spec: https://go.dev/ref/spec#Integer_overflow
type OverflowAction
  = PanicOnOverflow
  | TwoComplementWrap


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// OverflowDisposition<IRCarrier> — the per-language operation-
// semantics carrier for Go integer arithmetic (D2a(3)). Conj record
// (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - debug_default     the default OverflowAction for Go integer
//                       built-in operators in **debug** builds
//                       (canonically TwoComplementWrap per Go spec
//                       §Integer_overflow — same as release for these ops)
//   - release_default   the default OverflowAction in **release**
//                       builds (canonically TwoComplementWrap)
//
// Per the D2 row D2.5: overflow/UB/arbitrary-precision are DISTINCT
// std/ carriers selected by the alias target + the per-language
// OverflowDisposition carrier, never a re-declared semantics. This
// record carries Go's runtime-action selection; the abstract-vs-
// bounded-integer-semantics axis is selected via the alias target once
// std/integer.dag lands (e.g. bounded `Int32` vs arbitrary-precision
// `Int`).
//
// Floats / complex use separate operation-semantics carriers (NaN/Inf
// handling per IEEE-754, complex component rounding) — those rows land
// when the float / complex aliases land (pending std/float.dag T-3
// Wave-A3). The integer-overflow case is the canonical instance of the
// D2a(3) shape; float/complex mirror structurally with different fields.
type OverflowDisposition<IRCarrier> {
  debug_default: OverflowAction
  release_default: OverflowAction
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Per-primitive alias-identity + grounding declarations.
//
// Each Go primitive type below is declared as a thin three-fact
// resolver: (1) alias-identity to its std/ carrier, (2) grounding-
// map for surface spelling, (3) operation-semantics carrier (for
// integers, OverflowDisposition; for floats/complex, a future NaN/Inf /
// rounding disposition carrier; Bool/String/Never have no per-op
// runtime dispatch and don't carry one — string DEFERRED per the
// grounding-state note above).
//
// LANDABLE NOW — grounded against current origin/main carriers:

// Go `bool` ↔ std/logic.dag's `Bool` (LANDED via #3166).
// Alias-identity: literal Node identity per the D2 row's
// "see through the labels" framing.
type GoBool = Bool

data go_bool_grounding: GroundingMap<GoBool> = GroundingMap {
  spelling: "bool"
}

// Bool has no overflow / runtime-dispatch operation semantics; no
// OverflowDisposition row.


// 🟡 TRACKED-SCAFFOLD — DEFERRED ALIAS-IDENTITY ROWS
//
// The alias-identity rows below (D2a(1)) are 🟡 TRACKED-SCAFFOLD —
// shape ratified, landing blocked on named upstream substrate, with
// explicit owner + trigger + form contract so the deferral is a
// tracked-scaffold not a silent gap. Per manager directive
// msg_2c2716dd 2026-05-16 and the encoded DECISIONS.md D2 row.
//
// FORM CONTRACT — for each deferred alias, the form is the encoded
// DECISIONS.md D2 row (commit 44a37ad46, line 57). Land as:
//   - `type Go<X> = <StdCarrier>` alias-identity (D2a(1));
//     algebra inhabitance flows through the alias per the
//     `List<T> = FreeMonoid<T>` precedent — no parallel-substrate
//     per-language `OrderedRing<Go<X>, …>` inhabitance
//     (INVARIANTS P1:42 `numeric_aliases_align_to_refinements`).
//   - `data go_<x>_grounding: GroundingMap<Go<X>> =
//     GroundingMap { spelling: "<token>" }` row (D2a(2)).
//   - for integer aliases: `data go_<x>_overflow:
//     OverflowDisposition<Go<X>> = OverflowDisposition {
//     debug_default: TwoComplementWrap,
//     release_default: TwoComplementWrap }` row (D2a(3); **Go 1.26**
//     applies modular wrap for built-in ops on typed integer widths in
//     **all** build configurations — both fields canonically
//     `TwoComplementWrap`, unlike Rust's PanicOnOverflow debug default).
//   - for float aliases: a NaN/Inf disposition carrier row (the
//     carrier itself lands with std/float.dag's ApproximateField<Float>
//     inhabitance — same 🟡 TRACKED-SCAFFOLD deferral pattern,
//     float.dag as named owner).
//   - for complex types: component-wise float semantics + rounding
//     structure land with std/float.dag extensions for complex — NOT
//     authored here until that substrate exists.
//
// SUBSTRATE SINGLE-AUTHORITY GUARD — `go.dag` (and any sibling
// `rust.dag` / `python.dag` / etc.) MUST NOT mint the std/ Int / UInt /
// Float / pointer-width types locally. The abstract numeric carriers
// have SINGLE AUTHORITY at `std/` (substrate single-authority per
// INVARIANTS P2 + scaffold-drift discipline). The extdeps file's only
// contribution is the alias-identity to the std/ source — minting them
// locally would produce parallel substrates and break P2 enforcement at
// the carrier level.
//
// ─── DEFERRED ROWS — TRACKED ──────────────────────────────────────
//
// Go signed integers — GoInt8 / GoInt16 / GoInt32 / GoInt64 / GoInt
//   - target form        type GoInt8 = Int8 …
//                        + grounding `spelling: "int8"` etc.
//                        + OverflowDisposition row (Go §Integer_overflow
//                          — both defaults TwoComplementWrap)
//   - named owner        std/integer.dag — the bounded-width integer
//                        ladder + platform `int` width composition
//                        (per INVARIANTS:42 +
//                        `numeric_aliases_align_to_refinements` ratchet).
//   - dispatch wave      T-3 Wave-A3 (substrate spine integer carriers).
//   - dissolution trigger
//                        when the signed bounded ladder + `int`
//                        platform composition lands on `origin/main`
//                        in `std/integer.dag`, the rows above amend
//                        in as one-line landings each.
//
// Go unsigned integers — GoUint8 / GoUint16 / GoUint32 / GoUint64 /
//                        GoUint / GoUintptr
//   - target form        type GoUint8 = UInt8 …
//                        + grounding `spelling: "uint8"` etc.
//                        + OverflowDisposition row (same Go §Integer_overflow
//                          wrap-default for typed widths; `uintptr` follows
//                          the unsigned platform width substrate)
//   - named owner        std/integer.dag — the unsigned bounded-width
//                        ladder + `uint` / `uintptr` composition with
//                        std/machine.dag pointer widths.
//   - dispatch wave      T-3 Wave-A3.
//   - dissolution trigger
//                        same as signed — `UInt*` / `uintptr` ladder
//                        landing on `origin/main` in `std/integer.dag`.
//
// Go floats — GoFloat32 / GoFloat64
//   - target form        type GoFloat32 = Float32 …
//                        + grounding `spelling: "float32"` etc.
//                        + NaN/Inf disposition carrier row (carrier
//                          lands with std/float.dag).
//   - named owner        std/float.dag — the `Float` / `Float32` /
//                        `Float64` carriers and the `ApproximateField<Float>`
//                        algebra instance D2b's FunctionRef → Arrow
//                        IEEE-754 op declarations attach to.
//   - dispatch wave      T-3 Wave-A3.
//   - dissolution trigger
//                        when `std/float.dag` lands its Float32 /
//                        Float64 ladder + the ApproximateField<Float>
//                        inhabitance on `origin/main`, the rows above
//                        and the D2b realization surface below amend in
//                        together.
//
// Go complex — GoComplex64 / GoComplex128
//   - target form        alias + grounding (`"complex64"` /
//                        `"complex128"`) + disposition rows tying
//                        component IEEE-754 semantics to std/float.dag's
//                        complex extension.
//   - named owner        std/float.dag — complex is a structured
//                        composition over two IEEE-754 floats at each
//                        width; multiplication is rounding-approximate.
//   - dispatch wave      T-3 Wave-A3 + float/complex substrate closure.
//   - dissolution trigger
//                        when std/float.dag lands explicit complex
//                        carriers + ApproximateField structure for the
//                        Go complex widths on `origin/main`.
//
// Go string — GoString
//   - target form        type GoString = String + grounding
//                        (`spelling: "string"`).
//   - named owner        std/text.dag — `String = FreeMonoid<Char>` is
//                        LANDED but unrefined; Go strings are byte
//                        sequences (UTF-8 is idiomatic but not guaranteed
//                        by the spec) — a refinement / disposition
//                        question parallel to rust.dag's `str` deferral.
//   - dispatch wave      pending REFINEMENT-DISPOSITION decision (D2
//                          scope-guard ambiguity).
//   - dissolution trigger
//                        EITHER (a) std/text.dag refines the carrier
//                        to the Go-faithful byte-sequence / UTF-8
//                        interpretation split, OR (b) an operator-
//                        ratified per-language refinement-disposition
//                        carrier lands. Escalate when authoring the
//                        alias forces the decision.
//
// Go never — GoNever
//   - target form        type GoNever = <std/ Never carrier>.
//   - named owner        std/cardinality.dag — has Multiplicity but no
//                        explicit empty-cardinality / Never type yet.
//   - dispatch wave      pending std/ Never carrier authoring.
//   - dissolution trigger
//                        when std/ lands its Never / empty-cardinality
//                        carrier on `origin/main`.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// D2b — Go's IEEE-754 float + complex operation realizations.
//
// Per the D2 row D2b: IEEE-754 float arithmetic (and Go's complex
// types built from float pairs) are genuinely-irreducible spec
// primitives where applicable. They flow through THESIS:203's
// `Transform → FunctionRef → Arrow` pattern, where the Arrow's `body`
// is a REALIZATION DECLARATION in `extdeps/languages/*` — the per-
// target resolver. D2b coins NO new carrier (no `Symbol`+`Anchor`
// data node referenced directly by the inhabitance — P2 forbids the
// second external-primitive path).
//
// Per the D2 row's SCOPE GUARD, "D2 covers per-primitive inhabitance
// form ONLY: it does NOT decide LanguageModel-grammar facts." The
// Arrow-body realization data IS grammar/emission facts (Go source
// tokens for operators, precedence, associativity). Those land with the
// bundled T-4 LanguageModel grammar work; the D2 row directs the SHAPE
// (`Transform → FunctionRef → Arrow` with body = extdeps realization)
// but does not author the realizations themselves here.
//
// For this slice: the realizations are DEFERRED to the bundled T-4
// LanguageModel grammar bundle. They land alongside the broader grammar
// production data; not in this canonical reshape.
//
// (Also pending: std/float.dag's ApproximateField inhabitance for float
// and complex, which the FunctionRefs to op Arrows live INSIDE. Until
// std/float.dag lands its ApproximateField<Float> instance (and complex
// extensions) and the per-op Arrow declarations are bound to
// FunctionRefs, the D2b realization surface in go.dag has nothing to
// attach to.)
